# Routh I.Arts.350-355 — Lambert's theorem: the time depends only on the chord,
# the sum of the radii, and the axis — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Arts. 350-355 give Euler's theorem for the parabola and LAMBERT'S for
# the ellipse. Lambert's statement is the striking one: the time to travel from
# one point of an elliptic orbit to another depends on just three things --
#
#     the semi-major axis a,   the sum of the two radii r1 + r2,
#     and the chord c joining the two points
#
# and on nothing else. Not on the eccentricity, not on where the points sit
# relative to the apse line, not on the orientation of the orbit in space. In
# the standard form, with s = (r1 + r2 + c)/2,
#
#     t = sqrt(a^3/mu) * [ (alpha - sin alpha) - (beta - sin beta) ]
#     sin(alpha/2) = sqrt(s/(2a)),      sin(beta/2) = sqrt((s-c)/(2a))
#
# -- and every symbol on the right is one of the three permitted quantities.
#
# WHY IT DESERVES A TEST. It is easy to believe a formula and hard to believe
# this claim, because two orbits of the same a can look nothing like each other.
# So this notebook builds a genuine counter-intuitive pair: two ellipses of the
# SAME a but DIFFERENT eccentricity, with two points chosen on each so that
# r1 + r2 and c agree. Lambert says the transits must take equal times.
#
# THE PAIR. Both have a = 1, mu = 1. Solving for the second orbit's endpoints
# numerically (two equations, two unknowns) gives
#
#   orbit 1   e = 0.6    r1 = 0.5819759743917008   r2 = 1.3531006703532076
#   orbit 2   e = 0.35   r1 = 0.7437260742718752   r2 = 1.1913505704730332
#
#   both:     r1 + r2 = 1.9350766447449084     c = 1.2872743894793441
#
# The individual radii are quite different -- orbit 1 starts much closer in and
# ends much further out -- and only the SUM matches. Lambert's formula then
# gives, for both,
#
#     t = 1.3453158122479596
#
# agreeing to the last digit between the two.
#
# THE TEST. Each orbit is flown alone from its own starting point for exactly
# that time, and the arrival is compared with its own predicted endpoint:
#
#   orbit 1: start (0.09670670934716541, 0.5738848727196183, 0)
#            end   (-1.188501117255346,  0.6467971230556722, 0)
#   orbit 2: start (0.382211216366071,   0.6379992630370535, 0)
#            end   (-0.896715915637238,  0.784357538632282,  0)
#
# SETUP NOTES. A primary of mass 1e9 with G = 1e-9 makes mu = 1 exactly; each
# planet is given mass 1e-6 so it neither shifts mu nor perturbs anything.
# softening MUST be 0 -- Lambert's theorem is a property of the exact inverse
# square and of nothing else.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: two ellipses of the same size but different shape, each
# carrying a particle across an arc. The arcs look nothing alike. They take the
# same time.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_lambert_theorem
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_lambert_theorem.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_lambert_theorem.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- orbit 1 (e = 0.6) flown alone
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.09670670934716541, 0.5738848727196183, 0], velocity = [-1.2326214869081589, 0.9577119881285612, 0] }
norm(obj1.position)
run 1.3453158122479596 steps 3
norm(obj1.position)
norm(obj1.position - [-1.188501117255346, 0.6467971230556722, 0])
reset

# ---- orbit 2 (e = 0.35) flown alone, for exactly the same time
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.382211216366071, 0.6379992630370535, 0], velocity = [-0.9157640844146201, 0.9222463226977163, 0] }
norm(obj1.position)
run 1.3453158122479596 steps 3
norm(obj1.position)
norm(obj1.position - [-0.896715915637238, 0.784357538632282, 0])
reset

# ---- the scene: both orbits together
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
set system.softening = 0
set system.g_constant = 1e-9
new point { mass = 1e9, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.09670670934716541, 0.5738848727196183, 0], velocity = [-1.2326214869081589, 0.9577119881285612, 0] }
new point { mass = 1e-6, position = [0.382211216366071, 0.6379992630370535, 0], velocity = [-0.9157640844146201, 0.9222463226977163, 0] }

scene create
scene zoom in
scene set_time_step 0.01
